Consider the class of soft amorphous materials: disordered viscoelastic solids — including emulsions, polymer and colloidal gels, microgels, foams, concentrated particulate suspensions, and similar yield-stress fluids — that under sufficient mechanical forcing undergo a yielding transition from a solid-like response to a liquid-like flowing state. Empirically, this transition may appear "ductile" (a gradual, spatially homogeneous fluidization with smooth stress evolution) or "brittle" (an abrupt macroscopic stress drop, often accompanied by fracture-like or shear-banded localization).

Provide a predictive characterization of the ductile-to-brittle transition in such materials. Specifically, identify and quantify how the following control variables determine whether yielding is brittle or ductile, and at what threshold the crossover between the two regimes occurs:

(a) intrinsic material properties (elastic moduli, interaction potentials, attraction strength, polydispersity, particle/network architecture);
(b) preparation history and aging (quench protocol, age, prior shear and memory imprinting);
(c) confining geometry and boundary conditions (gap size, wall roughness, slip, free surfaces);
(d) deformation protocol (imposed strain rate, stress ramp rate, oscillatory amplitude and frequency, creep);
(e) microscopic-to-mesoscopic processes (plastic rearrangements, shear transformation zones, elastic interactions, damage accumulation, cavitation, strain localization).

Develop a framework — connecting microscopic dynamics to macroscopic constitutive response across the relevant length and time scales — that predicts, for a given soft amorphous system and protocol, whether yielding will be brittle or ductile, and that identifies the order parameter(s) and control parameter(s) governing the transition.

